import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `String` - Implementation-String.md parts 3 and 8, and Implementation-Storage.md part B's sink.
   ==========================================================================================

   Two implementations and one signature, exactly as `Array(a)` has. Nothing below this point is
   reachable from a program that has not written a string; nothing above it knows which target it is
   on.

   The guiding principle of that document is that the *default* path - length, indexing, append,
   concat - costs what the target's own string costs. That is why `length` is a field read rather
   than a walk, why `String.unit` does no decoding, and why the code-point tier (`Char`, `chars`, the
   boundary functions) is in `Char.yana` rather than folded into these: it is parts 4 to 7, and every
   one of them is layered *on* this without changing it.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why the names are namespaced under `String`

   Because `reserve`, `push` and `unit` are `Array`'s, and a string wanting the same three used to
   spell them `reserveString`, `pushString` and `stringUnit` - the type written into the name to get
   out of the way of a bare one already taken. `String.reserve` is that fact said once, and
   `s.reserve(n)` finds it from the receiver's type, which is what the awkward spelling was standing
   in for all along.

   `Array`'s are deliberately *not* namespaced. The namespace is for the second claimant of a name,
   not the first: bare `push` is what a container's append means, code generic over `Array(a)` calls
   it by that name, and moving it would be churn that buys the reader nothing.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   What a native string's fields are

   The two records below are what a `String` is: `data String {units: Array(CodeUnit)}` is the
   owner, and `StringView` after it is what a *shared* one is. Which of the two a body's
   `self.units` reaches is the position: `&self: String` and an owned one are the owner,
   `self: String` is the view. Analysis-Manifestations.md Part 8 is the design, and the whole of
   what the compiler holds about it is the two roles, `Viewed`, and the fact that a literal is a
   `String`; every field named in this file is the library's.
-}

{-
   What a native `String` is made of - the owner, one growable array of this target's code units.

   `@builtin(string)` is what binds it: the compiler holds no name for a string's content, and
   `StringType::content` is filled from this row. The wrapper is not ceremony - `&Array(CodeUnit)`
   is a *slice*, which is the fixed point of the container design, and the string algorithms need
   the owner because growing is what they do, so `&self: String` is how they ask for it.

   In Core rather than in Native, where it was until the ownership stage read `lib/`. A string is a
   Core type: `Show`, `Format` and a dozen other files name one, and with the record in Native every
   one of them had to `import Native` to do it - free in the shipping stage, where `String` is a
   primitive the compiler registers, and fatal in the ownership stage, where it is an ordinary
   record that has to be in scope. What the unsafe import still guards is the act that deserves it:
   `stringLiteral`, which forges a string over bytes nothing validated, is Native's and stays there.
-}
@platform(native) @builtin(string)
pub data String {units: Array(CodeUnit)}

{-
   What a shared `String` is made of - the view, two words that own nothing.

   `@local` and `@builtin(stringView)`: a representation record on Analysis-Manifestations.md Part
   8's terms, the string's counterpart of `Array.native.yana`'s `ArrayView`. The ownership stage lays
   every shared `String` out as this and reads no field of it; the shipping stage keeps a shared
   string the owner's address and reads *only* the fields, built by `view` below wherever a body
   asks for one. Either way a program never writes the name - a shared string is written `String`.
-}
@platform(native) @local @builtin(stringView)
data StringView {units: %CodeUnit, length: Size}

{-
   The derivation - a shared `String` out of the owner, which is the one body whose subject is the
   owner rather than the view.

   Everywhere else a shared `String` *is* the view, which is exactly what makes this the one body
   that cannot take one: it reads the owner's fields to build the view, and the compiler reads the
   `Viewed` role to give `self` the owner's fields here. What comes out is rooted in the string by
   `source'` - a view is a loan of the owner's storage, said as two words.

   The two steps of the count are `length`'s below, for the same reason: the stored count is a
   `@bits(30) U32` and `Size` is `I64` here, and a cast straight between them is not one the lower
   IR accepts.

   `@inline(always)` because the body is two loads, smaller than the call to it, and the inliner does
   not see that: at a cold site it copies only bodies weighing `kColdBudget` or less, and this one
   weighs more at that tier than it costs in machine code. 162 cold sites in `test/base` called it,
   and at `-inline none` it was 5,033 calls and 160 KB (J170, J171). The other accessors of this
   shape - `elements`, `elementsMut`, `Length(String)`, `values` - are not marked, and that was
   measured: no call to any of them survives `balanced`, so the directive only moves them earlier,
   and a body grafted at conversion weighs more to the inliner than the call it replaced. Marked,
   they were `balanced` +1,536 bytes and `speed` +6,400 for the same time, and -24 KB only at
   `-inline none`.
-}
@platform(native) instance Viewed(String):
    @inline(always) fn view(self: source'String) -> source'String =
        StringView {units: self.units.run.items, length: (self.units.length :: Int) :: Size}

{-
   The code unit address a string is defined by - `Array.native.yana`'s `values` for a string, and
   for the same reason: the result is rooted in the string by `source'`, so a read through it is a
   read of the string and the string's own loan covers it.

   `self.units` here is the *view's* field, since `self` is a shared string: two words, of which this
   is the first.

   **Here rather than in `Native/Text.native.yana`**, which is where it was. `StringView` above is
   `@local`, so its fields may be named in this module and nowhere else; handing out a raw pointer
   is `Native`'s business and reading another module's private representation is not the same act.
   `stringBytes` in `CString.native.yana` is this address as bytes and is still behind that import,
   which is the half the argument was really about.
-}
@platform(native) @inline
pub fn units(self: source'String) -> %source'CodeUnit = self.units

{-
   How many native units the string holds - part 3's `.length`, O(1) on both targets.

   **This is not a portable number, on purpose.** It is UTF-8 bytes natively and UTF-16 code units on
   JS, so the same content can answer differently in the two builds - a trade part 3 makes explicitly
   in exchange for zero overhead, on the grounds that the *complexity class* is the thing that has to
   be uniform across targets and a constant factor is a documented platform fact. Anyone who needs
   the same number everywhere asks for `countChars` instead, which is part 6 and is O(n) on both.
-}
@platform(native) instance Length(String):
    -- The view's second word, which `view` above already read at this width.
    fn length(self: String) -> Size = self.length

@platform(js) instance Length(String):
    fn length(self: String) -> Size = hostStringLength(self)

{-
   One raw native unit - part 3's `String[i: Int]`.

   **A `CodeUnit` and not an `Int`**, so that a literal compared with one is checked as a unit: `","`
   converts, and `"é"` is refused because no one unit of either encoding is that character
   (Implementation-String.md part 1.1). `pushUnit` below takes one for the same reason.

   **A function and not an `Index` instance**, which is a real limitation rather than an omission and
   is worth stating where someone will look for the subscript. `Index` hands back a *borrow*, because
   `xs[i] = v` and `&xs[i]` are the operations that make a subscript worth having - and a string unit
   is not storage on both targets. On JS it is `charCodeAt`, a number computed from a host string
   that has no addressable units at all. Core's own note on the class says exactly this: it does not
   cover a container whose elements are computed rather than stored, and a weaker parent class that
   yields values is what such a container wants and is not written yet.

   So `s[i]` does not parse into this today; `String.unit(s, i)` is the accessor. When the read-only
   parent class lands, this becomes an instance of it and the spelling arrives with no change here.

   No decode, no validation that what comes back is a whole code point. Reading one unit is always
   safe *as a read*; it just might be one byte of a multi-byte sequence, exactly as indexing one byte
   out of a `[CodeUnit]` promises nothing about the larger structure it belongs to. Bounds are unchecked,
   which is the same tier `get` on an array is on and goes the same way when `checkBounds`
   (Implementation-Containers.md §15) lands.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn String.unit(self: String, index: Size) -> CodeUnit = *(units(self) + index)

@platform(js) pub fn String.unit(self: String, index: Size) -> CodeUnit = hostCharCodeAt(self, index)

{-
   Concatenation - part 8, which observes that this is *already* encoding-agnostic with no extra
   work: joining two strings never inspects their content, only their raw units, whatever those mean.

   **The native body appends into the left operand rather than sizing a third string.** Both operands
   are `->` - see `Append` in Core.yana - so the storage the left one sits in is this function's to
   keep, and appending to it is the sink below with the units already to hand. A `++` chain therefore
   allocates on the doublings rather than once per `++`, which is the difference between amortized
   O(n) and O(n^2) for `acc = acc ++ x` in a loop.

   A literal's run is borrowed, so the first append to one relocates it to the heap and copies; every
   append after that is a `copyMemory` into room that is already there. That is the shape
   `Text.native.yana` describes at `resize`, reached from the operator rather than only from `Show`.

   `rhs` is consumed like `lhs`: its run is released when this frame ends, which is where the units
   it held go. Joining something the caller means to keep is `copy(that) ++ other`.

   The JS body is the host's `+`, unchanged: a host string is immutable, so there is no storage to
   take over and the engine's rope is what makes the chain linear there. The consuming signature
   costs that target nothing.
-}
@platform(native) instance Append(String):
    fn ++(->lhs: String, ->rhs: String) -> String:
        let rightLength = length(rhs)

        let &->out = lhs
        String.appendUnits(out, units(rhs), rightLength)

        return out

@platform(js) instance Append(String):
    fn ++(->lhs: String, ->rhs: String) -> String = hostConcat(lhs, rhs)

-- And the identity, which makes `String` a `Monoid` and every fold in Join.yana available on it. One
-- body for both targets: the empty literal is a borrowed run over no bytes natively and `""` on JS,
-- and neither allocates.
instance Monoid(String):
    fn empty() -> String = ""

{-
   Equality, as a raw unit-wise comparison - part 3.

   *"Always correct regardless of encoding, since encoding is an injective function of content"*, so
   this is one of the few places the two targets provably cannot disagree: two strings are equal here
   exactly when they are equal there, whatever each is storing.

   The length test first is not just an optimization - it is what makes the loop's bound safe.
-}
@platform(native) instance Eq(String):
    fn ==(self: String, other: String) -> Bool:
        let count = length(self)
        if count != length(other) then return False

        return compareMemory(units(self), units(other), count) == EQ

    fn !=(self: String, other: String) -> Bool = !(self == other)

@platform(js) instance Eq(String):
    fn ==(self: String, other: String) -> Bool = hostStringEq(self, other)
    fn !=(self: String, other: String) -> Bool = !hostStringEq(self, other)

{-
   Ordering, raw unit-wise lexicographic - part 3's *default*, chosen for the same "no overhead"
   reason `.length` is raw.

   This one *can* diverge between targets, and part 3 says so rather than papering over it: on JS,
   UTF-16 code-unit order is not code-point order for supplementary-plane characters compared against
   certain BMP ones. Every ASCII comparison - which is the overwhelming majority of what anyone sorts
   - agrees on both. Code that needs guaranteed code-point order calls `compareChars` (`Char.yana`).

   `compareMemory` over the shared prefix and then the two lengths - the same two steps a unit loop
   is, with the first one vectorized. It answers the order of the first differing *unit*, so the
   native comparison agrees with `memcmp` because a unit is a byte here and not because bytes are
   what it compares.

   **The first register is compared here, and only the rest in `compareMemory`.** Most orderings a
   sort asks for are of words that differ in their first few bytes, and there the call into
   `compareMemory`, its block-loop bounds and its general three-way `compare` were most of the cost:
   `compareChars` per word pair in `test/bench/unicode` went from 3.9 ns to 1.4 at v2, 2.5 times ICU
   to 0.9, and from 2.1 ns to 1.4 over CJK, whose words are longer than a register. The load reads
   past the shorter text's end, which `compareMemory`'s tail already does on the same guarantee, and
   the lanes past it are cut off by `limit` rather than by a branch on the length, which measured 60%
   slower on this backend. The rest is a `@noinline` call so that this body saves no register for it.
   `String.test.native.yana` checks every length and difference position around the register edge.

   **Thirty-two units and not the whole register at `avx512`**, whose natural width is 64 bytes.
   This is one compare per call and latency is all it is: two 64-byte loads that each cross a cache
   line where a 32-byte one does half the time, and a comparison into a mask register, measured 1.2x
   the time of the 256-bit head on every corpus in `test/bench/unicode` (test/bench/avx512/findings.md
   §10). What a longer shared prefix costs is `compareMemory`'s, which is faster at 512 bits.
-}
@platform(native) instance Ord(String):
    fn compare(self: String, other: String) -> Ordering:
        let leftLength = length(self)
        let rightLength = length(other)
        let &shared = leftLength
        if rightLength < shared then shared = rightLength

        if shared > 0:
            -- At most 32 units at once, which is the whole register below `avx512` and half of it
            -- there - see the note above. A test of a constant, so only one arm is ever compiled in.
            let head = if vectorBytes > 32 then 32 :: Size else vectorBytes
            let same = if vectorBytes > 32
                then bits((vectorPast(units(self)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 32)) .== (vectorPast(units(other)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 32)))
                else bits(vectorPast(units(self)) .== (vectorPast(units(other)) :: Vec(CodeUnit)))
            let differs = trailingZeros(not(same)) :: Size
            let limit = if shared < head then shared else head
            if differs < limit then return if *(units(self) + differs) < *(units(other) + differs) then LT else GT

            if shared > head then return compareAfterRegister(self, other, shared, head)

        return compare(leftLength, rightLength)

-- `compare` past the first block, which every unit of agreed.
@platform(native) @noinline
fn compareAfterRegister(self: String, other: String, shared: Size, head: Size) -> Ordering:
    let order = compareMemory(units(self) + head, units(other) + head, shared - head)
    return if order != EQ then order else compare(length(self), length(other))

@platform(js) instance Ord(String):
    fn compare(self: String, other: String) -> Ordering:
        if hostStringLt(self, other) then return LT
        if hostStringLt(other, self) then return GT
        return EQ

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The units between two positions - Implementation-String.md part 4's slice.
   ==========================================================================================

   Half-open and clamped rather than checked, on the same terms `unit` reads: `String.slice(text, 0,
   length(text) + 1)` is the whole string, and an empty range in either order is the empty string.
   Nothing here can read past the end.

   **What comes back depends on the resolve stage**, which is why all four bodies are in files of
   their own. Under `-resolver ownership` (`String.ownership.native.yana`) it is a **window**: a
   `StringView` over the same units, rooted in `text` by `source'`, with no allocation. That is the
   form part 4 specifies, and a caller that keeps the result past `text` says `copy`. The legacy stage
   (`String.legacy.native.yana`, `String.legacy.js.yana`) cannot represent a window over part of a
   string, because its shared `String` is the owner's address, so there it is a **copy**. Code
   written against the window is correct against the copy; the reverse is not true, so library code
   is written to the window.

   On JavaScript the two stages differ in the signature and agree on the body: `String.ownership.js.yana`
   declares the window and returns the host's own copy, which Analysis-IR-JS.md §3.2.2 is the
   argument for - a host string has no units to point into, so a window there would be three values
   carried everywhere to save a copy at the few places that cut.

   **Positions are raw units and this does not check that either lands on a code-point boundary.**
   Cutting between the two halves of one produces a unit sequence that decodes as U+FFFD. Every
   position the ASCII search family hands back is a boundary, because an ASCII unit is
   self-synchronizing in both encodings; any other offset is rounded with the boundary functions
   Implementation-String.md part 4 plans.
-}

{-
   Hashing a string - Implementation-Map.md §3 and §3.1.

   **One body for both targets**, which is unusual in this file and is the honest state rather
   than a claim about the two: what a string hash costs is the walk over its units, and both targets
   walk. `String.unit` is one load natively and one `charCodeAt` on JS, and `../../benchmark/map-js`
   §9.1 measures that walk as the *whole* cost there - reading the characters and merely adding them
   costs 30.9 ns per key where FNV-1a costs 24.0, so every candidate mixer lands within 12% of every
   other and the mixer is not what to spend on.

   The **length goes in first**, through the state fold, so that the two strings a prefix relation
   separates cannot fold to one value however the units happen to land - and so that the one
   expensive operation in this instance, the multiply-fold natively, happens once rather than per
   unit. What runs per unit is FNV-1a's step: one exclusive-or and one 32-bit multiply, which is
   `Math.imul` on JS and a `lea`-sized `imul` natively.

   **This is not §3.1's native row, and the gap is worth naming.** That row reads a string eight
   bytes at a time and folds each word with a 64x64 multiply, which is where its 1.97x on a string
   lookup comes from. Doing that here needs an *unaligned* sixty-four-bit load out of a run of bytes,
   and the language exposes no such load: `*p` at a `%U64` claims an alignment a string's bytes do
   not have, and the one overreading load there is (`vectorPast`) is a vector's and carries §8's
   tail-read guarantee with it. Closing it is a `Native` primitive and a fixture, not a change here.

   `mix32` in the map is what finalizes this, which is what lets the per-unit step stay this cheap:
   the seven-bit tag is taken from the *top* of the finalized word, so a fold whose entropy sits low
   is repaired once per lookup rather than once per unit.
-}
instance Hash(String):
    fn hash(value: String, state: U32) -> U32:
        let n = length(value)
        let &h = hashWord(state, truncate(n) :: U32)
        for i in 0 .. n:
            h = (h `xor` (String.unit(value, i) :: U32)) * 16777619

        return h

{-
   Releasing a string - Implementation-Containers.md §2's placement switch, and nothing else.

   An authored `Reclaim` rather than derived glue, because `String` is a primitive in the shipping
   stage and has no members for a derived walk to recurse into. What it does is exactly `releaseRun`:
   hand the slots back if and only if they are the allocator's. A literal's run is `runBorrowed`,
   so this folds to nothing for one; a concatenated or formatted string's is `runFromHeap`, so it
   frees once.

   A `Reclaim` and not a `Drop`, which is what keeps a string region-placeable: there is no effect at
   last use, only storage, and storage is exactly the kind of thing a region discharges in bulk.

   No JS instance at all, deliberately. The collector owns every host string, so there is nothing to
   release and no traversal to run - a string has no elements whose lifetimes could end in something,
   which is the one thing §14 says does *not* fold away for a container. That asymmetry is the whole
   of the difference, and it is why this is `@platform`-split rather than a body with a branch in it.

   Implementation-Simplification.md §17 left open whether this should become `Array(CodeUnit)`'s derived
   glue now that the bytes *are* an array. It should not, and the reason is what that glue contains:
   a walk of every element, and then `releaseRun(value.run)`. At `CodeUnit` the walk provably does nothing
   - a byte's move is a copy and its teardown is empty - so the optimized build would fold it back to
   the line below, and the unoptimized build would run a loop over every byte of every string. The
   line below is what the glue reduces to, written directly, and `String.yana.lower.expect` shows it
   reaching the machine as the placement test and a `freeHeap` with no call left at all.
-}
@platform(native) instance Reclaim(String):
    fn reclaim(->value: String): releaseRun(value.units.run)

{-
   Duplicating one - `Core`'s `Copy`, and what `++` consuming its operands makes routine.

   A string owns its run, so it is not TrivialCopy and the blanket instance beside the class does not
   reach it. Without this one there was no way at all to get a second `String` from a first, which is
   what made `a.copy() ++ "b"` - the escape hatch for joining something the caller does not own - a
   sentence that did not compile.

   The native body is `ofCapacity` and one `appendUnits`, which is the concatenation above with one
   source instead of two: sized exactly, one allocation, and the units copied without being looked
   at. A borrowed run - a literal - is copied to the heap by this, which is the point: the result is
   a string somebody may append to, and the original is left as it was.

   The JS body is the value. A host string is immutable and has no storage to share, so two names for
   one of them cannot observe each other and there is nothing to duplicate. That is not an
   optimization of a copy; it is what a copy *is* on that target - which is why under the rewrite
   there is **no JS instance at all**: a record over one host handle with no teardown is
   `TrivialCopy`, and the blanket instance beside the class is the same identity this would write.
   Writing one would *subtract* that classification, which is what an authored `Copy` does, and a
   string that is not trivially copyable needs storage at every borrowed position - including the
   member-name argument of a host operation, where the literal then reaches the emitter as a read of
   a temporary rather than as the constant it was written as. `String.legacy.js.yana` keeps it,
   because the shipping stage's JS string is a compiler primitive rather than this record.
-}
@platform(native) instance Copy(String):
    fn copy(from: String) -> String:
        let count = length(from)

        let &fresh = String.ofCapacity(count)
        String.appendUnits(fresh, units(from), count)

        return fresh

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The sink - Implementation-Storage.md part B, whose `Storage(StringUnit)` is this.
   ==========================================================================================

   That document's sink was a `Storage(a)`, the primitive Implementation-Containers.md §0 demoted;
   what replaced it is "the target's growable string", which is `String` itself. So formatting builds
   the string *in place* and finishing it is a reinterpretation rather than a copy, which is the
   property part 7 gives as the reason not to abstract over sinks in the first place.

   Three operations, and each has a native body that manages a run and a JS body that is one
   concatenation. The bound machinery is real natively and is inert on JS, where the host owns growth
   and there is no capacity to reserve - which is not a gap: `showBound` still decides *sizes*, and a
   host that ignores the reservation still gets the right answer, just without the allocation it
   saves.
-}

-- A string with room for `capacity` units and nothing in it yet. The run is the allocation; the
-- length is zero until something appends.
@platform(native) @builtin(stringOfCapacity) pub fn String.ofCapacity(capacity: Size) -> String =
    String {units: Array {run: newRun(capacity), length: 0}}

@platform(js) @builtin(stringOfCapacity) pub fn String.ofCapacity(capacity: Size) -> String = ""

{-
   Room for `wanted` more units, growing if there is not - the check-and-grow that
   Implementation-Storage.md part 8's strategy (c) appends through.

   Geometric, so that a format whose bound was `Nothing` still appends in amortized constant time
   rather than reallocating per unit. `resize` is what relocates, and a *borrowed* run - a literal
   being appended to - relocates by copying and freeing nothing, which is where copy-on-write happens.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn String.reserve(&self: String, wanted: Size):
    -- The container's own growth, rather than a second copy of it here. `reserve` takes the
    -- capacity to reach and this takes the amount to add, which is the whole difference between the
    -- two. The count is read before the array is lent out, because an exclusive loan of `self.units`
    -- is what `reserve` takes and a read of it cannot overlap one.
    let have = self.units.length :: Size
    reserve(self.units, have + wanted)

@platform(js) pub fn String.reserve(&self: String, wanted: Size): {}

{-
   One unit appended - what every `Show` instance ultimately writes through.

   Natively this is a store and a count bump, with the reservation hoisted out by whoever is about to
   write a known number of units. Part 7's contract is what makes that safe: an instance writes at
   most `showBound` units, the buffer was sized from the bounds, so the appends are provably in range
   and the reserve above them is the only check.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn String.pushUnit(&self: String, unit: CodeUnit):
    -- `push` is the reserve, the bounds check, the store and the count bump, and it was all four of
    -- them written twice until the bytes became an `Array(CodeUnit)`.
    push(self.units, unit)

@platform(js) pub fn String.pushUnit(&self: String, unit: CodeUnit):
    self = hostConcat(self, hostFromCharCode(unit))

-- A whole string appended, which is the common case and is a block copy rather than a loop.
@platform(native) @builtin(stringPush) pub fn String.push(&self: String, other: String):
    String.appendUnits(self, units(other), length(other))

@platform(js) @builtin(stringPush) pub fn String.push(&self: String, other: String):
    self = hostConcat(self, other)

{-
   The block copy both of the above are written in terms of. Private to this section: it takes a raw
   address, so it is exactly as unsafe as `copyMemory` and exactly as unreachable from a program.

   The check is the same one `push` makes and is here for a worse reason. `push` compared the count
   against the capacity and gave up, which lost an element; this copies a whole block and had no
   comparison at all, so a `String.reserve` the allocator refused was followed by a `copyMemory` of
   `count` units into a buffer that had not grown to hold them. That is a write past the end of the
   run, not a lost append - and it was reachable by exactly the route the array's was, since both
   reserves end at the same `resize`.

   Read off `run.capacity` rather than off `String.reserve`, which answers nothing: what has to hold
   before the copy is that the room is *there*, and that is one comparison against the field the copy
   is about to run past.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn String.appendUnits(&self: String, from: %CodeUnit, count: Size):
    if count <= 0 then return
    String.reserve(self, count)

    let have = self.units.length :: Size
    let wanted = have + count
    checkCondition((self.units.run.capacity :: Size) < wanted)

    -- The units as bytes, for `copyMemory`, which counts in bytes and takes them - see the note in
    -- `Core/Format.yana`'s `print`. `byteSpan` is generic over the pointee and needs no such thing.
    copyMemory(bitcast(self.units.run.items + have) :: %U8, bitcast(from) :: %U8, byteSpan(from, count))
    self.units.length = truncate(wanted) :: Count
